From dc2aef9ba47db1e233839b968798aee051914df9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 05:31:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/20] relay_metrics: Count HSDir fetches per descriptor and
 minute

Whether a flood fetches a few onion service descriptors again and again,
sweeps many of them, or asks for keys that miss calls for different
fixes: a limit per descriptor, a limit per circuit or source, or a limit
on misses. HSDirDescriptorFetchRate's count covers only descriptors in
the cache, and only at its own rate.

Count each fetch that reaches the cache, by whether it found the
descriptor (lookup "found" or "not_found", as
tor_relay_hsdir_fetch_total says) and by its place among the fetches of
the same blinded key in the same minute, in the bands the source counts
use, as tor_relay_hsdir_fetch_descriptor_total. Decode the key once,
before the lookup, so that every spelling of a key in the URL counts as
that key (base64 decoding stops at the first "="), and count a key that
doesn't decode as a first fetch. Keep only a keyed hash of each key
(siphash24g, whose key is random at each start) with its count, which
stops at the last band, in a 64 KB table that the first fetch of each
new minute clears. A key that finds no slot within 16 of its own counts
as a first fetch, so a minute with more distinct keys than the table
holds undercounts repeats, which only happens when the fetches are a
sweep. Nothing per key is exported. Bridges don't count these, nor does
a relay without a MetricsPort, the only place they are shown. Only
dircache.c, which is built only with the relay module, calls the
counter, so it needs no stub.

Part of #41339.
